<h2>AI Is Moving from &quot;Something You Open&quot; to &quot;Something That Is Always With You&quot;</h2>

<p>According to a January 2026 report by MediaPost, one of Meta's key strategic priorities in 2026 is &quot;expanding consumer-facing AI products&quot; [MediaPost, 2026.01]. Alongside the 6th edition of Andreessen Horowitz's &quot;Top 100 Gen AI Consumer Apps&quot; [a16z, 2026.04] and the wave of Big Tech AI product releases in early 2026 [PYMNTS, 2026.04], a clear pattern is emerging.</p>

<p>AI applications are undergoing a generational migration: from &quot;conversational tools opened through a browser&quot; to &quot;intelligent companions embedded in everyday devices.&quot;</p>

<h2>The Evolution of Product Forms</h2>

<h3>Smart Glasses: Meta's AI Hardware Core</h3>
<p>PCMag's 2026 review ranks Meta's Ray-Ban smart glasses among the best products tested [PCMag, 2026.04]. The key advance isn't the hardware itself, but AI capabilities deployed on the device — real-time translation, visual recognition, scene awareness — all running without relying on the cloud.</p>

<p>This means AI is extending from a &quot;chat interface on a phone screen&quot; to a &quot;first-person perspective&quot; wearable. The potential impact of this shift is greater than current market expectations suggest — every person's field of vision could become an AI input channel.</p>

<h3>AI Integration in Social Platforms</h3>
<p>Meta's core advantage lies in the massive user scale of its social platforms. Integrating AI capabilities into everyday apps like Facebook, Instagram, and WhatsApp reduces the &quot;barrier to use&quot; of AI to nearly zero: users don't need to deliberately open an AI product — AI is already embedded in the products they use every day.</p>

<h2>Competitive Landscape: Differentiation in Consumer AI</h2>

<p>The current consumer AI product market is forming several clear competitive paths:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Platform AI</strong> (OpenAI, Google, Meta): Leveraging massive user bases to integrate AI into existing product matrices.</li>
  <li><strong>Vertical AI</strong> (Perplexity, Midjourney, ElevenLabs): Achieving exceptional experience in a single scenario, acquiring users through word-of-mouth.</li>
  <li><strong>Embedded AI</strong> (Ray-Ban Meta, Humane, Rabbit): Packaging AI into physical devices, emphasizing &quot;AI interaction without screens.&quot;</li>
</ul>

<p>The business models for these three paths differ significantly: platform AI relies on user scale and advertising/subscription monetization, vertical AI depends on paid user conversion, and embedded AI follows hardware profit margins. Currently, no single path has proven its sustainability in the consumer market.</p>

<h2>Privacy and Trust Challenges</h2>

<p>CNBC reported in April 2026 that Meta's legal outcomes &quot;could have far-reaching implications for AI research and consumer safety&quot; [CNBC, 2026.04]. When AI devices can continuously &quot;see&quot; and &quot;hear&quot; users' surroundings, consumer acceptance of data collection scope and trust in privacy protection will become key determinants of consumer AI product adoption.</p>

<p>The essence of this issue is: what compromises between convenience and privacy are consumers willing to accept? Consumers in different markets and cultural backgrounds may answer this question very differently. This reminds us that &quot;localization&quot; of AI products involves not only language and interface but also alignment with privacy expectations and regulatory environments.</p>

<h2>The Human-AI Fit Perspective</h2>

<p>From consumers to organizations, AI's embeddedness in daily life is deepening. This means &quot;human-AI fit&quot; is no longer just an organizational issue — it is becoming a personal issue for everyone.</p>

<p>As AI transitions from &quot;a tool you use&quot; to &quot;a companion you coexist with in your environment,&quot; the capabilities needed are also shifting:</p>
<ul>
  <li>From &quot;learning to use AI tools&quot; to &quot;learning to evaluate the reliability of AI outputs&quot;</li>
  <li>From &quot;choosing one AI platform&quot; to &quot;managing parallel use of multiple AI ecosystems&quot;</li>
  <li>From &quot;reading AI privacy policies&quot; to &quot;proactively managing AI data access permissions daily&quot;</li>
</ul>

